.TH CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_MEMPOOL_BUFFER 3 "2026-08-28" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_MEMPOOL_BUFFER \- declare buffer for memory pool
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_MEMPOOL_BUFFER(name, elem_count, elem_size);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares a uint8_t array sized correctly for a memory pool with the given
parameters, so that the caller can pass the buffer to
ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(). The size covers the stride
of each element and the status byte of each element; the pool keeps those
status bytes in the same buffer, after the entries.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name        Variable name for the buffer
elem_count  Number of elements that the pool holds
elem_size   Size of each element in bytes
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The buffer goes on the stack or into static storage, depending on context
This is useful on embedded systems, and whenever you do not want a heap
  allocation
The macro declares the buffer with the alignment that every entry the
  pool hands out is guaranteed to meet, which is 16 bytes. You can
  therefore pass the buffer directly to
  ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer() without having to
  think about alignment
The 16 is a fixed constant, not _Alignof(max_align_t), because the macro
  sets the size of an array in the translation unit of the caller while
  the library calculates its own layout from the same value. The two
  sides can be built by different compilers, which can disagree about
  _Alignof(max_align_t). A _Static_assert makes sure that 16 is enough
  for the real requirement of the target
An elem_size smaller than sizeof(uintptr_t) is rounded up to hold the
  free-list pointer. The macro does this rounding before it computes the
  buffer size, in the same way as
  ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(), so the declared buffer
  always holds exactly elem_count elements once you pass it to that
  constructor with the same elem_size
The macro rounds the resulting stride of each element up to that same
  alignment, and then to a power of two unless both the library and this
  translation unit were built with -DCCOL_MEMPOOL_COMPACT_LAYOUT=1. That
  setting must match the setting of the library, and the build checks
  it: a buffer declared here names an object that the library defines
  under the name of the setting it was built with, so a mismatch becomes
  an undefined reference at link time (to
  _ccol_mempool_built_with_fast_layout or to
  _ccol_mempool_built_with_compact_layout) instead of a wrongly sized
  buffer at run time. The two settings differ only for an element size
  that is not already a power of two. In both cases the stride matches
  ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer() exactly, so every
  element in the resulting pool, not only the first one, is aligned for
  any object type
The macro reserves one status byte for each element after the elements,
  in the same buffer, so the pool needs no separate allocation of its
  own
elem_count must be nonzero. A _Static_assert rejects two cases at
  compile time: an elem_count of zero, and an elem_count times the
  rounded and aligned elem_size that would overflow size_t
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_MEMPOOL_BUFFER(my_buffer, 100, 64);
ccol_mempool *mp = ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(
    my_buffer, sizeof(my_buffer), 64, false, false, NULL, NULL);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer (3)
